  "faqs": [
    {
      "q": "What are the steps in a RAG pipeline?",
      "a": "Load documents, split them into chunks with metadata, index the chunks (keyword search, embeddings or both), retrieve the most relevant chunks for a question, add them to a prompt with clear rules, generate an answer that cites them, and evaluate retrieval and answers on a fixed test set."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I build a RAG pipeline in Python without LangChain?",
      "a": "Yes. A basic pipeline needs only a way to split text, a way to search it (TF-IDF, BM25 or embeddings) and a model call. Building it in plain Python first helps you understand what frameworks such as LangChain or LlamaIndex do for you."
    },
    {
      "q": "Do I need a vector database to build RAG?",
      "a": "Not to start. Keyword search or an in-memory index works for small collections. A vector store such as pgvector or FAISS becomes useful when you have many documents and want meaning-based search, usually combined with keyword search."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the best chunk size for RAG?",
      "a": "There is no single best size. Start with natural units such as paragraphs or sections, keep metadata with each chunk, and compare a few options on your own test questions. Measure retrieval hit rate rather than guessing."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I stop a RAG chatbot from hallucinating?",
      "a": "Set a minimum retrieval score, instruct the model to answer only from the provided context and to say \"I don't know\" otherwise, require citations, and check faithfulness on a test set. Better retrieval (hybrid search, reranking) reduces hallucinations more than prompt tweaks alone."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between RAG and fine-tuning?",
      "a": "RAG gives the model new information at question time by retrieving documents; fine-tuning changes the model's behaviour by training it on examples. Use RAG for knowledge that changes or must be cited, and fine-tuning for consistent style or format. Our RAG vs fine-tuning guide covers this in detail."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I evaluate a RAG pipeline?",
      "a": "Build a test set of real questions with the source passage that should answer each one. Measure retrieval (is the right chunk in the top results?) separately from generation (is the answer supported and relevant?). Tools such as Ragas offer metrics like context precision, context recall and faithfulness."
    }
  ],
